From: mscharged-port
Subject: Keep the online lobby's outgoing connection as its word
Upstream-base: 9046e67aa2077a7eb4ed6e28c9df3c1d3b368f84
Classification: native compiler/ABI compatibility

NetworkLobby::UpdatePeerConnectionState passes &mPlayers[aid].mConnection,
an unsigned int, to NetworkSocket::Connect, and ReliableSocket::Connect
stores the new TransportConnection through it as a void*. On the Wii both
are four bytes. Natively the eight-byte pointer kept its low half in
mConnection and overwrote mAid with the high half, so the stored value
was not the encoded word (0476) the transport callbacks report:

- OnConnected never matched the peer, which stayed connecting until
  "Lobby Start game timed out" failed the match after five seconds;
- CloseConnections then decoded that value and stopped with "Connection
  word is outside Wii cached memory" (with 0744) or crashed (without).

LANLobby's slot is a TransportConnection*, so ReliableSocket::Connect
keeps its contract. Natively the lobby receives the pointer in a local
and stores its word; a failed connect leaves the word 0 as before.

diff --git a/src/Game/NetworkLobby.cpp b/src/Game/NetworkLobby.cpp
index 27a104a..930c288 100644
--- a/src/Game/NetworkLobby.cpp
+++ b/src/Game/NetworkLobby.cpp
@@ -15,6 +15,9 @@
 // Closing a peer passes the connection itself, decoded from its word.
 #define NETWORK_LOBBY_CONNECTION_POINTER(word) \
     mscharged::platform::DecodeTransportConnectionAddress(static_cast<std::uint32_t>(word))
+// An outgoing connection is returned as a pointer and kept as its word.
+#define NETWORK_LOBBY_CONNECTION_WORD(pointer) \
+    mscharged::platform::EncodeTransportConnectionAddress(pointer)
 #else
 #define NETWORK_LOBBY_CONNECTION_AID(word) (*((u8*)(word) + 0x27))
 #define NETWORK_LOBBY_CONNECTION_POINTER(word) (TransportConnection*)(word)
@@ -840,7 +843,16 @@ void NetworkLobby::UpdatePeerConnectionState(int aid)
         address[3] = aid;
         NetworkSocket* socket =
             g_pNetworkSessionBase->GetDirectSocket();
+#if defined(MSCHARGED_NATIVE)
+        // ReliableSocket::Connect writes the connection through a
+        // pointer-sized slot; mConnection is a 32-bit word.
+        TransportConnection* connection = 0;
+        bool connecting = socket->Connect(&connection, address, 0);
+        mPlayers[aid].mConnection = NETWORK_LOBBY_CONNECTION_WORD(connection);
+        if (connecting)
+#else
         if (socket->Connect(&mPlayers[aid].mConnection, address, 0))
+#endif
         {
             tDebugPrintManager::Print(DC_NETWORK,
                 "Attempting Reliable connection to AID %d...\n", aid);
